//! The rule table of this crate: one [`RuleDescriptor`] per rule id it can report.
//!
//! This file is data, not logic. It is the single place where a rule id acquires its kind,
//! its severity and a one-line summary, so that a rule id cannot silently acquire the wrong
//! severity at a call site. The table is deliberately kept one descriptor per source line and
//! in a fixed field order, because `scripts/check_rule_ids.py` reads it with a line-oriented
//! parser: it fails when a specification rule here is not a rule id of the SBML specification
//! checkout, and it fails when the table cannot be parsed at all.
//!
//! The other direction is checked by `scripts/check_emitted_ids.py`, which collects every rule
//! id the sources can pass to the issue constructor and fails when one of them is missing
//! here: `SbmlIssue::new` panics for an unregistered id, and that panic would otherwise wait
//! for the path that reports it. Both checks run in `make rules` and `scripts/verify.sh`.
//!
//! The summaries are adapted from the SBML specification; see the crate documentation for the
//! attribution. They are the first sentence of the rule text, so they are a pointer to the
//! rule, not a replacement for it. A rule whose first sentence is longer than one line is cut
//! short and ends with `…` (108 of the 216 specification rules); the summary of such a rule is
//! a pointer to the specification, and the full text is the place to read the requirement.

